WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Why can a moth population change colour even though no moth changes?

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.740
Peppered moths rest on trunks and branches during the day.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.340
Some are pale and speckled.

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:13.720
Others are nearly black.

5
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.340
We will follow them through a woodland.

6
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:25.413
The moths in this wood form a population: the same kind of living thing in one area.

7
00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:29.333
Each moth keeps its inherited wing colour for life.

8
00:00:30.653 --> 00:00:32.813
New variation first appears by chance.

9
00:00:34.253 --> 00:00:37.353
Natural selection works with variation already present.

10
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:41.233
Watch the blue-black and cream moths become parents.

11
00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:46.973
Their offspring usually share their colour because genes pass from parents to young.

12
00:00:48.393 --> 00:00:51.093
Real moths receive colour genes from both parents.

13
00:00:52.353 --> 00:00:55.273
This picture uses a simpler parent-to-offspring match.

14
00:00:56.283 --> 00:01:00.463
Now the bark darkens with soot after pollution kills pale lichen.

15
00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:06.783
The environment includes everything around the moths, including bark, air, food and

16
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.403
predators. A bird enters.

17
00:01:10.783 --> 00:01:13.923
It spots moths that stand out from the background more easily.

18
00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.633
On this dark bark, which colour will birds find more often?

19
00:01:19.873 --> 00:01:22.413
Which colour will leave more young for the next generation?

20
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.020
The pale moths stand out, so the bird finds more of them.

21
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.840
Dark moths are missed more often.

22
00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.080
More dark moths survive to reproduce.

23
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.160
To reproduce means to have young.

24
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.280
The survivors do not become better camouflaged.

25
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.740
They already had their colour.

26
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.220
Move the survivors into a parent row.

27
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.380
Their offspring fill the woodland again.

28
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.440
A generation is the step from parents to offspring.

29
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.980
The new row contains a larger share of dark moths, although every individual kept

30
00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:05.980
its original colour.

31
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.320
How common a trait is in a population is called trait frequency, often written as a

32
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.260
percent. Advance the woodland through more generations.

33
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.040
On this sooty bark, the dark share usually grows as the same difference repeats.

34
00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:26.053
Now clean the air and let pale lichen grow back.

35
00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:28.453
The bark becomes lighter.

36
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:33.393
Dark moths now stand out more, so birds remove a larger share.

37
00:02:34.653 --> 00:02:35.933
The direction can reverse.

38
00:02:37.313 --> 00:02:41.333
Dark moths became rarer again in Britain and parts of the United States.

39
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:47.837
A trait shaped by natural selection that helps survival and reproduction is an

40
00:02:47.837 --> 00:02:52.817
adaptation. Dark colour helped on sooty bark, not pale bark.

41
00:02:54.017 --> 00:02:56.497
Whether a trait helps depends on the environment.

42
00:02:57.917 --> 00:03:01.577
Moving individuals can pass genes into another population.

43
00:03:02.697 --> 00:03:04.937
That is gene flow, a different process.

44
00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.870
This is natural selection.

45
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:14.430
Individuals whose inherited traits suit their environment are more likely to survive

46
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:20.950
and reproduce. Inherited traits that help survival and reproduction become more

47
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.310
common over generations.

48
00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.750
The rule describes a population, not a moth changing.
